-- Q3529: Data Management and Lifecycle [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Degaussing is too expensive to be practical for solid-state drive media","SSDs use flash memory cells unaffected by magnetic fields, so degaussing fails","Degaussing works perfectly well on SSDs and is the recommended approach","SSDs cannot store sensitive customer data requiring secure destruction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1, degaussing is effective only for magnetic storage media. SSDs store data using electrical charges in NAND flash memory cells, which are completely unaffected by magnetic fields. Organizations must use cryptographic erasure, ATA Secure Erase, or physical destruction.
Wrong (A): Cost is not the reason degaussing fails on SSDs. The issue is physical incompatibility — magnetic fields have no effect on flash memory technology.
Wrong (C): Degaussing has zero effect on SSDs. NIST SP 800-88 explicitly states that degaussing is not a valid sanitization method for flash-based storage.
Wrong (D): SSDs routinely store sensitive data including financial records. The issue is the destruction method, not the storage medium''s data handling capability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3529;

